I think Slashem provides a very important role in the nethack
ecosystem. Sure, some (many, actually) of the added features are
overpowered, frivolous, unbalanced or totally redundant, but at least
they are being given a chance. Changes to the 'official' nethack tend
to be incredibly conservative and glacially incremental, and I'm not
going to argue with anyone who says that it shoud stay that way.
However, if there's a seperate and parallel branch where radical ideas
an be tried out, then I don't see anything wrong with that at all.
While it's true that you don't need a whole new branch to playtest a
patch, few patches have the level of appeal and audience that slashem
has. You can be sure that any worthwhile idea within slashem will be
thoroughly used, abused, stretched, beaten, broken, debated, argued and
championed by a large number of users, so if there are any gems in
there worthy of migration back across to vanilla (and there have been
in the past, and are probably more now), then you can be satisfied that
they will be pretty well-polished by the time they get there.

Rather than some kind of crude, brutal copy of nethack, think of
slashem as an anarchic playground where all kinds of big, boisterous
ideas are allowed to bounce off one another and develop into the
refined and cultured modifications that make nethack great.

